WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.614
Hi, I'm Elena.

2
00:00:01.614 --> 00:00:09.516
I'm the EL specialist from the Every Teacher podcast, and this is a ten-minute session for the teacher who teaches content, not English.

3
00:00:09.516 --> 00:00:14.746
Biology, algebra, history, PE — if you have English learners in the room, this is for you.

4
00:00:15.246 --> 00:00:20.433
We're going to do three things. Get clear on the two words on your roster, Emerging and Progressing.

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.701
See what a student at each level can actually do in your class.

6
00:00:23.701 --> 00:00:28.109
And then — this is the part that matters — walk through what you change for each one.

7
00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:31.170
Not two different lessons. One lesson, two different sizes.

8
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:32.669
First, the words.

9
00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:38.195
Every spring, every English learner in Mississippi takes the E L P T — the state English test.

10
00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:43.486
It gives a score of one to five in four domains: listening, speaking, reading and writing.

11
00:00:43.486 --> 00:00:49.423
A student who is mostly at levels one and two is called Emerging. Mostly three and four, Progressing.

12
00:00:49.423 --> 00:00:54.185
Level five across the board, and the student is proficient and exits the program.

13
00:00:54.685 --> 00:00:58.770
Here's the first thing I want you to do with that. Don't stop at the label.

14
00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.459
Ask for the four domain scores.

15
00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:10.209
A student who is a four in speaking and a two in writing needs something very different from you than a student who is the reverse — and both of them might be labeled Progressing.

16
00:01:10.209 --> 00:01:14.730
The EL teacher has the scores. The Language Service Plan has them on page one. Ask.

17
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.466
Now, what can an Emerging student do? More than you'd think.

18
00:01:18.466 --> 00:01:23.374
They can follow a routine they've seen before. They can point, match, sort, copy and label.

19
00:01:23.374 --> 00:01:26.017
They can say a single word or a memorized phrase.

20
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:29.901
They can answer a yes-or-no question, or a question with a choice in it.

21
00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:32.058
With a frame, they can write a sentence.

22
00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:37.020
And — this is the big one — they very often know the content already, in their own language.

23
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:46.352
What they can't do yet is follow you talking at normal speed for ten minutes, read a paragraph of the textbook, write a paragraph alone, or answer an open question out loud.

24
00:01:46.352 --> 00:01:50.019
That's a language gap. It's temporary. It is not a learning problem.

25
00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:54.878
A Progressing student is the one most teachers underestimate in the other direction.

26
00:01:54.878 --> 00:01:59.445
They sound fluent in the hallway. They joke, they argue, they've got the social English.

27
00:01:59.445 --> 00:02:08.318
They can read a grade-level text with support, write a paragraph if you give them a word bank and a structure, explain their thinking out loud, give a reason with because.

28
00:02:08.318 --> 00:02:21.187
Where they stall is the dense stuff: the thirty-word textbook sentence, the word problem with the question buried in the middle, the passive voice — energy is stored — the connectors like however and as a result, and the language of test questions.

29
00:02:21.187 --> 00:02:25.650
And because they sound fluent, nobody notices. They just look like they aren't trying.

30
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.898
So there's a trap for each one. With an Emerging student, the trap is assuming they can't do the content.

31
00:02:31.898 --> 00:02:34.197
They can; they can't do it in English yet.

32
00:02:34.197 --> 00:02:38.138
With a Progressing student, the trap is assuming that fluent means fine.

33
00:02:38.138 --> 00:02:43.721
Social English is not academic English, and the gap between the two is exactly what your class is for.

34
00:02:44.221 --> 00:02:49.854
Part one. What you change for the Emerging student. Five things. None of them is a different lesson.

35
00:02:50.354 --> 00:02:51.177
Here they are.

36
00:02:51.677 --> 00:02:53.039
One. Five words first.

37
00:02:53.039 --> 00:03:00.530
Before the lesson, the five words it falls apart without, each with a picture, said out loud by everyone, used ten times.

38
00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.201
Not thirty words on Monday.

39
00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:07.752
Two. Show everything twice. Say it and point. Say it and the diagram is on the slide.

40
00:03:07.752 --> 00:03:13.635
Say it and write the key word as you say it. Diffusion — walk across the room and spread your arms.

41
00:03:13.635 --> 00:03:15.299
Two channels for every idea.

42
00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:20.954
Three. Sentence frames with a word bank next to them. This is a blank cell — plant, animal.

43
00:03:20.954 --> 00:03:24.411
The frame carries the grammar; the student supplies the idea.

44
00:03:24.411 --> 00:03:27.754
A level one can join a discussion they'd otherwise sit out.

45
00:03:28.254 --> 00:03:34.191
Four. Check by showing, not by asking. Point to the nucleus. One finger for plant, two for animal.

46
00:03:34.191 --> 00:03:39.583
Sort these cards. Draw it in three boxes. A student who can't say it can usually show it.

47
00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:42.279
Five. Use the home language as a bridge.

48
00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:45.902
Write the Spanish cognate next to the English word — célula, cell.

49
00:03:45.902 --> 00:03:48.428
Hand out a bilingual glossary for your course.

50
00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:58.145
And give the student a partner whose job is to show, slower, with the picture — not to translate, because if the partner translates, the newcomer never has to listen in English.

51
00:03:58.645 --> 00:04:04.390
And what does output look like for an Emerging student? Labels on a diagram. A list of four parts.

52
00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:10.546
Two or three sentences from frames. A sort, a match, a drawing. One word said to a partner. That is work.

53
00:04:10.546 --> 00:04:15.002
It counts. And you grade the content fully — did they label the parts right?

54
00:04:15.002 --> 00:04:19.927
That's biology, and that's full credit. The English you note, and you fix one thing.

55
00:04:19.927 --> 00:04:22.682
Same standard as everyone else; smaller output.

56
00:04:23.182 --> 00:04:24.891
Part two. The Progressing student.

57
00:04:24.891 --> 00:04:28.611
Also five things, and they're different, because the problem is different.

58
00:04:28.611 --> 00:04:32.984
This student doesn't need the door opened. They need to be pushed through the next one.

59
00:04:33.484 --> 00:04:34.348
Here's the list.

60
00:04:34.848 --> 00:04:40.472
One. Unpack one sentence a day. Pick the textbook sentence you know will stop them, before class.

61
00:04:40.472 --> 00:04:45.749
Who does it, does what, to what, using what. Rewrite it as three short sentences, together.

62
00:04:45.749 --> 00:04:48.880
You're teaching them to read science — nobody else is.

63
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.182
Two. Frames that push, not frames that rescue. Although blank, the evidence shows blank.

64
00:04:54.182 --> 00:04:58.819
I think blank because blank, however blank. They would not produce that on their own.

65
00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:01.984
With the frame, they will — and after a month, without it.

66
00:05:02.484 --> 00:05:02.840
Three.

67
00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:09.903
Teach the words that live in every subject and no textbook glossary: analyze, consequence, despite, whereas, therefore.

68
00:05:09.903 --> 00:05:10.794
Tier-two words.

69
00:05:10.794 --> 00:05:16.670
Your Progressing students know photosynthesis; they don't know whereas, and whereas is on the test.

70
00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:22.241
Four. Talk before writing. Speaking is ahead of writing for almost every Progressing student.

71
00:05:22.241 --> 00:05:26.113
A minute with a partner saying the answer, then two minutes writing it.

72
00:05:26.113 --> 00:05:28.185
If they can say it, they can write it.

73
00:05:28.685 --> 00:05:31.515
Five. Wait time. Count to five after a question.

74
00:05:31.515 --> 00:05:35.820
The Progressing student spends the first second translating the question.

75
00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.591
And pick who answers — not the same four hands.

76
00:05:39.091 --> 00:05:47.808
Output for a Progressing student is a paragraph with structure, connectors — because, however, as a result — an explanation out loud, a claim with evidence.

77
00:05:47.808 --> 00:05:49.149
Grade the content fully.

78
00:05:49.149 --> 00:05:54.681
Give language feedback on one pattern at a time: this week it's past tense, next week it's because.

79
00:05:54.681 --> 00:05:57.307
Say it to them in person. Don't just circle it.

80
00:05:57.807 --> 00:06:01.710
Part three, and this is the whole point. You don't teach two lessons.

81
00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.917
You teach one, and you size the output.

82
00:06:04.417 --> 00:06:06.724
Here's a cell-parts lesson, both lanes.

83
00:06:06.724 --> 00:06:14.592
Emerging lane: five words with pictures — nucleus, membrane, cytoplasm, mitochondria, cell — núcleo and membrana written beside them.

84
00:06:14.592 --> 00:06:20.390
Label the diagram with a partner. Frame: the blank controls the cell. Check: point to the nucleus.

85
00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:25.655
Progressing lane, same lesson: the same five words plus function, structure, and whereas.

86
00:06:25.655 --> 00:06:29.263
Unpack the sentence about the selectively permeable membrane.

87
00:06:29.263 --> 00:06:34.765
Frame: unlike the blank, the blank blank, whereas. Talk for a minute, then write a paragraph.

88
00:06:34.765 --> 00:06:40.030
Check: explain to a partner why a plant cell needs a wall. Same forty minutes. Same cell.

89
00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:42.988
Two sizes of English in, two sizes of English out.

90
00:06:43.488 --> 00:06:48.859
So here's your planning routine, ten minutes before any lesson. Five words — and which are cognates.

91
00:06:48.859 --> 00:06:52.886
One frame per lane on the board. One check that needs no English to answer.

92
00:06:52.886 --> 00:06:54.873
The size of the output for each lane.

93
00:06:54.873 --> 00:07:04.701
And a glance at the accommodations written in each student's Language Service Plan, because those aren't suggestions — and because somebody already wrote down what this student needs.

94
00:07:05.201 --> 00:07:06.067
One last thing.

95
00:07:06.067 --> 00:07:12.361
Everything I just said is one rule, sized two ways: English in and English out, every day, for every student.

96
00:07:12.361 --> 00:07:17.096
For an Emerging student the in is a picture and five words and the out is a label.

97
00:07:17.096 --> 00:07:23.043
For a Progressing student the in is an unpacked sentence and the out is a paragraph with however in it.

98
00:07:23.043 --> 00:07:26.969
Nobody sits for ninety minutes and produces nothing. That's the job.

99
00:07:26.969 --> 00:07:29.164
You already know how to teach biology.

100
00:07:29.164 --> 00:07:33.090
This is just teaching it in a way a learner of English can get into.

101
00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:35.039
Thanks for ten minutes.

102
00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:46.441
The tools for all of this — the frame bank, the cognate hunt, the accommodations cards, the LSP builder — are free in the PD Library at radan55 dot github dot io slash everyteacher.

103
00:07:46.441 --> 00:07:47.953
Go pick your five words.
